            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Chapter 2 — The Invisible Living World: Beyond Our Naked Eye"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.893,
            0.03,
            0.918,
            0.045
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "23"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.086,
            0.346,
            0.109
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ever heard of ..."
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.125,
            0.556,
            0.229
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Spirulina, a microalga, is called a superfood because of its health benefits. Spirulina is also a good source of vitamin "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "\\mathbf { B } _ { 1 2 ^ { \\flat } }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " which is essential for our body. It is rich in protein—more than 60 per cent of its body weight—and only a small amount of fat and sugar."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.56,
            0.119,
            0.74,
            0.224
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.56,
                0.119,
                0.74,
                0.224
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_15_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.23,
            0.751,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Nowadays, farming of Spirulina is becoming a feasible livelihood opportunity. You can grow Spirulina easily by following these steps:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.268,
            0.748,
            0.287
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Set a clear glass tank in a bright place away from direct sunlight."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.287,
            0.749,
            0.321
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Cover the tank with a shade net, or keep the tank at a place with moderate temperature conditions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.139,
            0.322,
            0.439,
            0.339
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Fill the tank with pond water."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.339,
            0.545,
            0.375
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Add living Spirulina collected from a pond."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.377,
            0.528,
            0.395
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Stir the growing Spirulina twice a week."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.138,
            0.395,
            0.546,
            0.447
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. After 3–6 weeks, Spirulina may be harvested from the tank by filtering it through a fine cloth."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.549,
            0.348,
            0.744,
            0.444
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 13,
              "bbox": [
                0.549,
                0.348,
                0.744,
                0.444
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_15_image_13.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.137,
            0.446,
            0.75,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Conservation of microalgae is a good practice for ensuring food security and livelihood development."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.75,
            0.39,
            0.852,
            0.498
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 15,
              "bbox": [
                0.75,
                0.39,
                0.852,
                0.498
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_15_image_15.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.515,
            0.74,
            0.566
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2.5 Why Is Cell Considered to Be a Basic Unit of Life?"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.578,
            0.744,
            0.709
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The body of all living organisms are made up of tiny building blocks called cells. A single cell contains various components that help organisms perform various functions. The bodies of all plants and animals are made up of many cells. Therefore, they are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "multicellular",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (many-celled) organisms. In multicellular organisms, cells carry out specialised functions individually but also cooperate with each other to increase the chance of survival."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.711,
            0.549,
            0.879
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Some microorganisms, such as bacteria and protozoa, are made up of just one cell. These are called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "unicellular",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " (single-celled) organisms. They carry out all the functions necessary for their survival in a single cell. Other microbes, like algae and fungi, are made up of one or more cells. For example, yeast is a unicellular fungus while mould is a multicellular fungus."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.756,
            0.71,
            0.832,
            0.727
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Nucleoid"
            }
          ]
        },
